.TH BJORN 1 "2026-09-25" "bjorn 0.1.0" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
bjorn \- terminal front end for the Bear note app
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bjorn
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B bjorn \-\-list\-themes
.br
.B bjorn
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-demo ]
.B capture
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ text ...]
.br
.B bjorn
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-demo ]
.B today
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bjorn
is a terminal client for Bear (https://bear.app), written in Rust with
ratatui. Every read and every write goes through
.BR bearcli ,
the command line tool that ships inside Bear.app, run as a subprocess; Bjorn
never opens Bear's database and never makes a network call of its own. That is
why it works with Bear open or closed, and why anything that has to reach the
network \(em a model, an upload, a deploy \(em is an
.I action
you configure rather than something built into the binary.
.PP
The screen is three columns, like the app: smart views and a nested tag tree on
the left, the notes list in the middle, the rendered note on the right.
.B tab
cycles them and
.B c
hides them one at a time. Editing is delegated to
.BR $VISUAL ,
then
.BR $EDITOR ,
then
.BR vim ,
which runs in a pseudo\-terminal drawn inside the reader pane so the other two
columns stay live. Writes back are hash\-guarded: if the note changed in Bear
while you were editing, nothing is written and your version is kept in a temp
file.
.PP
Views are computed from one
.B bearcli list
snapshot, so the counts in the sidebar and the notes list always agree. A
background poll (see
.IR poll_seconds )
runs two cheap probes together and reloads only when something changed. Every
.B bearcli
and
.B remctl
call has a 30 second timeout.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-tag " TAG"
Start scoped to this tag as the workspace, overriding
.I workspace
in the config file. A leading
.B #
is optional.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Read this config file instead of
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/bjorn/config.toml .
A leading
.B ~
is expanded.
This is also the file the action menu writes to, and
.I .theme
files are then read from
.I themes/
beside it.
.TP
.B \-\-demo
Run against a built\-in fake
.B bearcli
seeded with sample notes, so nothing touches your library and Bear need not be
installed. Reminders are forced on and backed by a fake
.BR remctl ,
and the preview cache gets its own file
.RI ( previews\-demo.json )
so the sample library never stands in for the real one.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " NAME"
Draw with this palette, overriding
.I theme
in the config file. The name is a built\-in or one of your own theme files (see
.BR THEMES ).
A name typed here was typed just now, so an unknown one is
an error that lists the names and the directory searched, and a theme file that
exists but does not load is named with the reason. An unknown name in the
config file is not: it falls back to the default with a warning inside the app.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-themes
Print the names
.B \-\-theme
accepts, one per line, and exit: the built\-ins, then your own theme files.
A theme file that does not load is reported on standard error with the reason.
This runs before the config file is read.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-mouse\-pixels
Accepted and ignored. The Rust build never negotiates pixel mouse reporting;
the mouse always stays in cell mode. The config key
.I mouse_pixels
is accepted and ignored in the same way.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
Both commands work on today's daily note and need a
.B [daily]
table in the config (see
.BR "DAILY NOTES AND TEMPLATES" ).
Without one they print
.I Daily notes are off \(em add a [daily] section to config.toml
on standard error and exit 1, before reading standard input or calling
.BR bearcli .
.BR \-\-config ,
.B \-\-demo
and
.B \-\-tag
go before the command; after it, any word starting with
.B \-
is an error rather than text.
.TP
.B capture \fR[\fB\-\-\fR] [\fItext\fR ...]
Add a line to today's daily note, making the note first if needed. The words
are joined with spaces; with none, the text is read from standard input, up to
1 MiB. The line is
.I capture_format
filled in, by default
.BR "* 14:05 text" ,
added at the end of the note or under
.IR capture_section .
Control characters other than tab and newline are dropped. A multi\-line
capture stays one entry: blank lines are dropped and the lines after the first
are indented past the bullet, so a pasted heading or rule stays text. Prints
nothing on success. Write
.B bjorn capture \-\- \(dq$text\(dq
for text that might start with a dash.
.TP
.B today
Print today's daily note as
.IR id <TAB> title ,
making it first if needed.
.SH KEYS
These are the bindings the in\-app help
.RB ( ? )
lists, in the order it groups them. Letters typed into a search box, a filter
or a form go to that box, which is why the menus use
.B ctrl
for their commands.
.SS Move
.TP
.B tab / ⇧tab
cycle the panes
.TP
.B j k ↑ ↓
move within a pane; in the reader they scroll
.TP
.B g / G
first / last row
.RB ( Home
and
.B End
do the same;
.B PageUp
and
.B PageDown
page the reader)
.TP
.B enter
open the highlighted note in the reader, at the first match while searching
.TP
.B 1 – 7
Notes, Untagged, Todo, Today, Pinned, Archive, Trash
.TP
.B c
cycle the columns: hide tags, then notes too, then show all three (or click
▮▮▮ in the note header)
.TP
.B r
refresh from Bear now
.SS Search
.TP
.B /
search with Bear syntax:
.BR @todo ", " #tag ", " \(dqphrase\(dq ", " \-term .
.B @
and
.B #
complete as you type;
.B tab
or
.B →
accepts. A bare sub\-tag is searched as
.B #*/name
.TP
.B esc
clear the search and its highlights
.TP
.B ] / [
next / previous match in the reader
.SS Sections
.TP
.B o
outline: the note's headings, indented by level, the current one highlighted
.IP
In the outline: type to filter ·
.B "↑ ↓ tab ctrl+n/p"
pick ·
.B enter
scrolls there ·
.B esc
closes
.TP
.B } / {
next / previous heading in the reader
.SS Notes
.TP
.B n
new note (title, tags), then edit
.TP
.B N
new note from a template in the templates directory
.RI ( "[templates] dir" ,
default
.IR ~/.config/bjorn/templates ):
pick one, then settle the title and tags as for
.B n
.TP
.B D
open today's daily note, making it from the daily template the first time.
Off until the config has a
.B [daily]
table; without one,
.B D
shows how to turn it on and makes nothing
.TP
.B e
edit in
.BR $VISUAL " / " $EDITOR .
Hash\-guarded: if the note changed in Bear meanwhile, nothing is written and
your version is kept in a temp file
.TP
.B d
move the note to the trash (asks first)
.TP
.B u
restore from Trash or Archive
.TP
.B p
toggle the global pin
.TP
.B b
open the note in Bear.app
.TP
.B x
export as Markdown
.RB ( m ),
HTML
.RB ( h ),
plain text
.RB ( t ),
RTF
.RB ( r ),
TextBundle
.RB ( b )
or PDF
.RB ( p );
.B ← →
pick,
.I export_format
in the config sets the default; a PDF needs a converter (see
.BR EXPORT )
.SS Actions
.TP
.B !
run the default action on the note, or open the menu if none is set
.TP
.B a
the action menu: your
.B [[actions]]
under a search box, ★ on the default, the highlighted command shown in
full
.IP
In the menu:
.B enter
runs ·
.B + New action
adds one ·
.B ctrl+e
edits ·
.B ctrl+d
