.SH VIEWS AND THE WORKSPACE
The sidebar's seven smart views are
.BR Notes ,
.BR Untagged ,
.BR Todo ,
.BR Today ,
.BR Pinned ,
.B Archive
and
.BR Trash ,
reachable with
.B 1
to
.BR 7 .
.B Pinned
means any pin, global or inside a tag;
.B Today
means modified today, local time. They are computed from the same
.B bearcli list
snapshot the notes list is drawn from, so the counts always agree.
.PP
The
.B workspace
is a tag subtree that scopes the whole app: the tag tree shows only it, the
smart views count only inside it, search results are filtered to it, todo
triage reads only it, and new notes default to it.
.B w
on a tag makes it the workspace and
.B w
again on the same tag leaves;
.B W
clears it. It can also be set for a run with
.B \-\-tag
or permanently with
.I workspace
in the config file. When you follow a link to a note the workspace excludes,
the workspace is cleared so the note can be shown, with a toast saying so.
.SH SEARCH
.B /
opens the search box and the query goes to
.B bearcli search
unchanged, so Bear's whole syntax works and plain terms match body text, not
just titles. While you type,
.B @
completes bearcli's operators
.RB ( @todo ,
.BR @title ,
.BR @last7days ,
.BR @date( \&...)
and
.B #
completes your tags, the workspace's first, as ghost text that
.B tab
or
.B →
accepts. A one\-line cheat sheet sits under the box.
.PP
Bear matches
.B #name
against full tag paths only, so a bare sub\-tag
.RB ( #Build
for
.BR #kybernetes/Build )
is completed and searched as
.BR #*/Build ,
Bear's sub\-tag form.
.PP
.B enter
runs the search and moves to the results; the box stays open with the query,
dimmed, until you press
.B /
or click it to edit again. While a search is active the reader highlights the
terms, including inside fenced code and table cells, the header counts the
matching blocks,
.B ]
and
.B [
step through them, and
.B enter
on a note lands on its first match.
.B esc
clears the search and its highlights.
.SH WIKI LINKS
Bear's
.B [[Note title]]
links are drawn in the theme's link color, without the brackets; click one to
follow it, in the text or in a table.
.B [[Note title/Heading]]
lands on that heading, and
.B [[Note title|shown text]]
shows the text after the bar. Bear escapes punctuation that belongs to the
title with a backslash
.RB ( \e/ ", " \e# ),
so a
.B \e
before any ASCII punctuation is one, and
.B [[/Heading]]
points into the note itself. Some links carry a doubled escape
.RB ( "[[Cloud Arch \e\e/ EA/Apr 19]]" ,
for the note
.IR "Cloud Arch / EA" );
a link is read as written first and then with each doubled escape taken as one,
and the first reading that names a note wins. Whatever is between the brackets
is the title, markdown or not; brackets in a title work as long as they pair up
.RB ( "[[[Draft] Plan]]" ),
but a title holding
.B ]]
cannot be linked. Brackets inside code, inline or fenced, are left as written.
.PP
A link resolves to the note with that exact title, ignoring case; when several
share it, an active note wins over an archived one, which wins over one in the
trash, and then the newest. A title no note has offers to create the note and
then opens it in the editor, as
.B n
does.
.PP
.B L
lists the links in the note (the first 1,000) and, below them, the notes that
link to it, under a search box;
.B enter
follows. Backlinks come from a
.B bearcli search
for the phrase
.BR [[Title ,
with the title's
.B /
and
.B #
escaped as Bear writes them, run in the background one search at a time. A
title with a
.B /
or a
.B #
is also searched with the escape doubled, and one with a
.B /
with the slash bare, since links are written all three ways. Each phrase is
searched in Notes and in the Archive separately. Bear
matches a phrase as a prefix, so every candidate's body is parsed and only real
links to this title are kept: not
.BR "[[Title 2]]" ,
not a mention inside code, nothing from the trash. At most 200 candidates are
read per search, and the list says so when that cap is hit. A title too short
or odd for a phrase (under three letters or digits before a
.BR \(dq ", " \e " or " | )
is searched as
.B @wikilinks
instead, which covers every note holding a
.BR [[ .
.PP
Following a link records where you were.
.B backspace
(or
.BR ctrl+o ,
.BR alt+← ,
.BR alt+b )
goes back, list, workspace, search and scroll included;
.B alt+→
(or
.BR alt+f )
goes forward again. While the search box is open,
.B backspace
edits the query instead.
.B enter
in triage counts as a jump too.
.SH TODO TRIAGE
.B t
opens a screen listing every open
.B "- [ ]"
item from the
.B @todo
notes in the workspace (all notes when none is set), grouped by note with the
section each item sits under.
.B space
marks rows,
.B x
ticks the marked (or highlighted) items in Bear through
.BR "bearcli edit" ,
.B enter
jumps to the note,
.B b
opens it in Bear.app at that section,
.B /
filters,
.B r
reloads, and
.B esc
or
.B q
closes.
.PP
With
.B [reminders] enabled = true
and
.B remctl
(https://github.com/7robots/remctl) on
.BR $PATH ,
.B a
also pushes the marked items to Apple Reminders, into
.I list
with the due date
.IR due .
Each reminder's notes carry the note's
.B bear://
link and a
.B "bear\-todo: <key>"
line, the same scheme remtui uses, so reminders it created are recognized. On
every load they are read back and rows show ⏰ for an open reminder
or ✓ for one completed in Reminders, ready to
.B x
in Bear. Nothing is written into Bear when a reminder is added.
.SH DAILY NOTES AND TEMPLATES
Bear has no daily notes of its own; its Today view is notes modified today.
Bjorn makes them only when the config has a
.B [daily]
table, and an empty one turns them on with the defaults.
.PP
.B D
opens today's note, making it the first time. By default it is titled with the
date as a second\-level heading and tagged with a dated nested tag:
.PP
.EX
## September 19, 2026 (Saturday)
#log/2026/09/19
* People:
* Topic:

\-\-\-
.EE
.PP
The note is found by its title: Bjorn selects it when the snapshot already has
it, and otherwise runs
.BR "bearcli create \-\-if\-not\-exists" ,
which returns the existing note or makes one. Asking twice never makes a second
note; two processes asking in the same instant could, and the duplicate\-title
warning then says so. A trashed or archived note with today's title is never
reused. Renaming the note's heading in Bear makes it no longer today's note,
and the next
.B D
or capture makes another.
.PP
Because the title is the only link,
.I title
must name exactly one day: a year with a month and day
.RB ( %Y\-%m\-%d ),
a year and day of the year
.RB ( %Y\-%j ),
or an ISO week date
.RB ( %G\-W%V\-%u ),
and no time of day. A title that repeats
.RB ( %A ", " "%B %\-d" )
would reuse last week's or last year's note, so Bjorn refuses it at start. The
check runs only while
.B [daily]
is on.
.PP
A capture belongs to the day it runs: one at 00:05 goes to the new day's note.
In a git hook,
.B capture
with no words reads git's own standard input, so pass the text as an argument
or add
.BR </dev/null .
.PP
.B N
makes a note from a template: a Markdown file in the templates directory. When
a template starts with a heading (after any YAML front matter, which stays on
top), its text is the proposed title. These placeholders are filled in, and
anything else in braces stays as written:
.TP
.B {{date}} {{time}}
today's date and the time now
.TP
.B {{date:\fIformat\fB}}
today formatted with any strftime format, e.g.
.B {{date:%A}}
.TP
.B {{title}} {{tag}} {{workspace}}
the note's title, its tags as Bear writes them, and the workspace
.PP
Files over 256 KiB or not UTF\-8 are skipped. A
.I daily.md
in the directory replaces the built\-in daily layout and is left out of
.BR N 's
list;
.B {{tag}}
in it is the dated tag. Meeting, one\-on\-one, decision and daily examples ship
in
.IR config/templates/ .
.SH EXPORT
.B x
writes the note under the cursor to a file you pick, in the format chosen in
the picker; an action's
.I format
renders the note the same way before its command sees it. Locked notes are
refused.
.PP
The picker offers Markdown
.RB ( m ),
HTML
.RB ( h ),
plain text
.RB ( t ),
RTF
.RB ( r ),
TextBundle
.RB ( b )
and PDF
.RB ( p );
.B \(<- \(->
move between them,
.I export_format
preselects one and
.I export_dir
is the folder proposed. RTF is written by macOS
.BR textutil (1)
from the HTML rendering; a note with images becomes an
.I .rtfd
package so the pictures travel with it.
.SS HTML
The page is self\-contained: the stylesheet is inline and every attachment is
embedded as a
.B data:
URI. Links and list markers wear the theme's link and bullet colors, mixed
toward the page until they read at 4.5:1; the rest of the page stays neutral,
light or dark as the browser asks, and always light when printed, on A4.
Bear's own marks come across: a colored highlight, a
.B "> [!NOTE]"
callout as a panel, a
.B [[note]]
link as its target in the accent color, a ticked task grayed out.
.B $math$
and the icon Bear draws inside a callout are not drawn.
.PP
The note's own inline HTML reaches the page as written, so the page carries a
Content\-Security\-Policy that refuses to run it in a browser: no scripts,
nothing fetched from anywhere, images only from
.B data:
URIs, styles only inline, and no
.B <base>
or form submission. A converter that ignores the policy (WeasyPrint does) still
fetches what the note's HTML points at, which is why the built\-in PDF filters
the note first.
.SS PDF
.B p
in the picker, or
.B "format = \(dqpdf\(dq"
on an action, prints the note to a PDF.
.B bearcli
does not export and nothing in Bjorn draws a page, so the HTML rendering, on A4
and always on a white page, is printed by a converter already on the machine.
Bjorn uses the first of these it finds:
.IP 1. 4
.BR weasyprint (1)
on
.BR PATH .
It runs no scripts and needs no browser.
.IP 2. 4
A Chromium browser, run headless:
.BR chromium ,
.B google\-chrome
or
.B google\-chrome\-stable
on
.BR PATH ,
then Google Chrome, Chromium, Brave Browser, Microsoft Edge or Vivaldi in
.I /Applications
or
.IR ~/Applications .
It gets a throwaway profile, so the print never sees your cookies and does not
fail because the browser is already open, and no network: no hostname
resolves and anything else meets a dead proxy.
.PP
macOS ships neither; without one the export says so and writes nothing. The
converter gets 60 seconds before it is killed, and an action's own
.I timeout
starts only once the PDF is written.
.PP
The built\-in PDF filters the note's HTML; piping a
.B "format = \(dqhtml\(dq"
action to a converter yourself does not. A note's inline HTML reaches the page
as written, and a converter fetches what the page points at: a remote image
tells somebody the note was printed, and a local one bakes a file off the disk
into the PDF. So before printing, Bjorn parses the note's body and rebuilds it
from an allowlist: no scripts, styles, frames, objects or SVG, no relative or
.B bear:
links, and an image only when it is a PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, BMP or TIFF already
embedded as a
.B data:
URI, which every attachment is by then. An attachment in another format (SVG,
HEIC, a PDF) prints as an empty frame.
.SH ACTIONS
An action is a shell command Bjorn runs against the note under the cursor.
Export writes the note to a file you chose; an action hands that same file to a
command of yours and reports what it said. What the command prints can also go
back into Bear.
.B !
runs the default action;
.B a
opens the menu. A query in the menu matches a name by its letters in order, so
.B pts3
finds
.IR "Publish to S3" ,
and a command by plain substring, so
.B curl
finds an action by what it runs. Locked notes are refused, as they are for
export.
.PP
Integrations live here rather than in the binary: a model through
.BR llm ,
.B claude
or
.BR curl ,
and publishing to a Hugo site through
.I contrib/hugo\-publish
(see
.BR EXAMPLES ).
.SS Config keys
Actions live in the config file as an array of tables,
.BR [[actions]] ,
in the order they are written. An entry without a
.I command
is skipped rather than raised, so a half\-written action never stops the app.
.TP
.I name
The name the menu shows and the toast reports. Default: the command.
.TP
.I command
Required. Run through
.BR "sh \-c" ,
so pipes,
.B &&
and redirection all work.
.TP
.I format
How the note is rendered first:
.BR md ", " html ", " txt ", " rtf ", " textbundle ", " pdf .
Any other value stops the action before it runs, so a typo is never handed a
Markdown file instead.
.B pdf
is printed and filtered as described under
.BR EXPORT ;
.B html
is the unfiltered page.
Default:
.BR md .
.TP
.I confirm
Ask before running. Worth setting on anything that publishes, deletes or costs
money. Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.I prompt
Ask for one line of text first and pass it as
.BR $BJORN_ACTION_INPUT ;
the value is the prompt's title. A blank or non\-string one is no prompt at all.
Default: none.
.TP
.I interactive
Give the command the window and the keyboard in a pseudo\-terminal instead of
capturing its output. Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.I timeout
Seconds before a captured command is killed and reported. Not applied to an
.I interactive
action. Default:
.BR 300 .
.TP
.I output
Where stdout goes:
.B toast
(its first line),
.B append
(to the end of the note, or of
.IR section ),
.B new\-note
or
.B replace
(the note's whole text). Any other value stops the action before it runs, so a
typo neither writes nor runs. Default:
.BR toast .
.TP
.I section
With
.BR "output = \(dqappend\(dq" :
the heading to add under, written as it is in the note
.RB ( \(dq##\ Summary\(dq ).
Blank or absent appends to the end of the note. Set on any other
.IR output ,
it stops the action from running. Default: none.
.TP
.I default
The action
.B !
runs. With exactly one action configured, that one is the default whether or
not it says so; setting a new one flips the older entry's
.BR "default = true" .
Default:
.BR false .
.SS What the command gets
The note is rendered exactly as export renders it and written to a temp
directory, which is also the command's working directory. The file is named
after the note
.RI ( "Sprint Planning.md" )
and is deleted as soon as the command ends.
.TP
.B stdin
The note's text in the chosen format. A TextBundle is a folder, so stdin gets
its
.IR text.md ,
and
.B pdf
puts the PDF's own bytes there, which a command that expects text should not
read.
An
.I interactive
action is the exception: its stdin is the terminal, so it reads the note from
.BR \(dq$BJORN_NOTE_FILE\(dq .
.TP
.B $BJORN_NOTE_FILE
The full path to that file. Quote it; titles have spaces.
.TP
.B $BJORN_ACTION_INPUT
What
.I prompt
collected; empty when there is no prompt, and empty when the prompt was
answered with nothing.
.TP
.B $BJORN_NOTE_TITLE
The note's title.
.TP
.B $BJORN_NOTE_ID
The note's Bear id.
.TP
.B $BJORN_NOTE_TAGS
The note's tags, comma\-separated.
.TP
.B $BJORN_NOTE_CREATED
Creation time, RFC 3339.
.TP
.B $BJORN_NOTE_MODIFIED
Modification time, RFC 3339.
.TP
.B $BJORN_NOTE_PINNED
.B 0
or
.BR 1 .
.TP
.B $BJORN_NOTE_FORMAT
The
.I format
the note was rendered in.
.TP
.B $BJORN_ACTION
The action's name.
.PP
The command inherits Bjorn's own environment too, so
.BR $PATH ,
.B $AWS_PROFILE
and anything else your shell exported when you launched Bjorn is there. It does
not read your
.IR ~/.zshrc :
it is
.BR "sh \-c" ,
not a login shell. If an action needs a shell function or an alias, put it in a
script and call the script. The variables reach the command through its
environment, never spliced into the command text, so a
.B ;
or a
.B $(\&...)
inside one is inert \(em unless you leave it unquoted, or hand it to something
that evaluates its input
.RB ( eval ,
.BR "sh \-c \(dq$VAR\(dq" ,
.BR "$(( VAR ))" ).
.SS Output modes
By default the command's output is a toast: its first line, and the rest is
dropped
.RB ( Done.
when it printed nothing). Exit status 0 is success; anything else is an error
toast with the exit code and the first line of stderr.
.I output
sends all of it back into Bear instead, through the same
.B bearcli
every other write uses.
.B append
adds it to the end of the note, or of
.IR section ;
a section the note does not have is an error and nothing is written.
.B new\-note
makes a note of the output, titled from its first line (after any YAML front
matter) and tagged the way
.B n
tags one.
.B replace
writes the output over the note's whole text: it always asks first, whether or
not the action says
.BR "confirm = true" ,
it is hash\-guarded the way the editor is, and it needs
.BR "format = \(dqmd\(dq" .
Bear cannot undo it, so the text the note had is saved to a temp file first and
the toast says where.
.PP
Output goes to Bear as it was printed, without the blank lines around it, with
.B \er\en
turned into
.B \en
and control characters other than tab and newline taken out.
.SS Interactive actions
.B "interactive = true"
gives the command the window and the keyboard in a pseudo\-terminal, titled
with the action's name, until it exits \(em the same machinery the editor uses.
It gets the same note and environment a captured action gets, with two
differences: stdin belongs to the terminal rather than to the note, and
.I timeout
does not apply, since the command is waiting on you. If Bjorn exits first, the
command is killed. One interactive action runs at a time. A captured action, by
contrast, runs in a process group of its own, away from the terminal, so a
command that tries to prompt there (an ssh passphrase, a login) gets no answer
and waits out its timeout; give those
.BR "interactive = true" .
.SS What stops a write
.TP
A non\-zero exit, a timeout or a failure to start.
The toast reports it and says nothing was written.
.TP
Empty output.
A command that printed nothing, or only blank lines, never makes an empty note
or wipes one.
.TP
More than 1 MB.
Refused rather than cut short; the first megabyte is kept in a temp file.
.TP
Output that is not UTF\-8 text.
Kept in a temp file as it came.
.TP
A note that went to the trash while the command ran.
Checked just before the write; the output is kept.
.TP
An entry that cannot work.
A
.I format
or an
.I output
value Bjorn does not know, an
.I interactive
action with an
.IR output ,
.B replace
with a format other than
.BR md ,
a
.I section
on anything but
.BR append .
The action does not run at all and a toast says why, and the add and edit form
will not save it.
.PP
When Bear refuses a write, the output is kept in a temp file and the toast says
where
.RI ( .../bjorn\-output\-XXXX/Summarize.md ),
so an answer that took a minute and cost money does not have to be asked for
again. The folder is readable only by you, and it lives under
.BR $TMPDIR .
.PP
An LLM command reads the note, and a note can hold text written to steer it.
Run such commands with their tools turned off, so the worst a steered answer
can do is be wrong.
.SH THEMES
The palette comes from
.I theme
in the config file, or
.B \-\-theme
on the command line;
.B \-\-list\-themes
prints the names and
.B ?
inside the app lists them and marks the one in use. Three are hand\-tuned:
.B red\-graphite\-dark
(the default),
.B red\-graphite
and
.BR textual\-dark .
Every other theme Bear ships is built in too, under its Bear name in lower case
with hyphens for spaces \(em
.BR academia ", " atom ", " ayu ", " dracula ", " gruvbox ", " nord ,
.BR rose\-pine ", " solarized\-dark ", " tokyo\-night
and the rest. Their colors are generated from Bear's own theme files by
.I tools/bear_theme.py
and committed to the repository; no build step or code path reads Bear.app, so
a Bear update cannot break a shipped Bjorn.
.PP
Your own themes are Bear\-format
.I .theme
files in
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:\-~/.config}/bjorn/themes/
(or
.I themes/
beside the file
.B \-\-config
names). A file's name, lower\-cased with hyphens for spaces and accents
folded, is its theme name:
.I My Nord.theme
is
.BR my\-nord .
Built\-in names always win, and naming a built\-in never reads the directory.
A file needs only
.BR "base.background color" ,
.B "base.text color"
and
.BR "base.accent color" ;
the rest falls back to the nearest color it has.
.B $section.key
references resolve, and
.B meta.\(dqbase theme\(dq
names a base looked up in the same directory and then among the built\-ins.
Only top\-level files are read, a symlink is followed to a regular file only,
and a file over 256 KB is skipped. Two files with one name resolve in file name
order.
.I config/themes/
in the repository has three hand\-written examples (Paper, Paper Night, Nord
Ember) and says where to copy Bear's own theme files from.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.IR "${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:\-~/.config}/bjorn/config.toml" ,
or the file named with
.BR \-\-config .
Every key is optional and the values below are the defaults.
.PP
The file is edited as
.IR text ,
never rewritten: adding, editing or deleting an action from the menu splices
the lines it has to change, so your comments, blank lines and commented\-out
templates stay exactly as they were. Every such write parses the file before
and after and reads the entries back through Bjorn's own parser, and refuses
unless the result is exactly what was intended.
.SS Top level
.TP
.I editor
The editor command. Default: empty, which means
.BR $VISUAL ,
then
.BR $EDITOR ,
then
.BR vim .
.TP
.I export_dir
Where
.B x
proposes to write. Default:
.IR ~/Downloads .
.TP
.I export_format
Preselected in the export picker:
.BR md ", " html ", " txt ", " rtf ", " textbundle ", " pdf .
Default:
.BR md .
.TP
.I poll_seconds
Seconds between background refresh probes;
.B 0
disables the poll. Default:
.BR 5 .
.TP
.I workspace
Start scoped to this tag. Default: empty.
.TP
.I bearcli
Path to
.BR bearcli .
Default: empty, which searches
.B $PATH
and then inside Bear.app.
.TP
.I icon_style
.BR auto ", " nerd ", " emoji ", " lucide ", " none .
Default:
.BR auto ,
which picks Nerd Font glyphs under Ghostty or WezTerm or when a Nerd Font is
installed, and emoji otherwise.
.TP
.I theme
The palette name. Default:
.BR red\-graphite\-dark .
An unknown name falls back to the default with a warning.
.TP
.I mouse_pixels
Accepted and ignored; the mouse always stays in cell mode. Default:
.BR true .
.TP
.B [icons]
A table mapping a top\-level tag to a Lucide icon name or
.BR emoji:<glyph> .
Default: empty, which uses the built\-in table in
.IR src/icons.rs .
.SS [reminders]
.TP
.I enabled
Let triage push todos to Apple Reminders. Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.I list
The target list, for example
.BR Bear .
Default: empty, which is remctl's own default.
.TP
.I due
The due date for new reminders;
.B \(dq\(dq
for none. Default:
.BR today .
.TP
.I remctl
Path to
.BR remctl .
Default: empty, which searches
.BR $PATH .
.SS [daily]
Present, even empty, turns daily notes on; absent, they are off. See
.BR "DAILY NOTES AND TEMPLATES" .
.TP
.I title
strftime format of the note's title, which is how the note is found; it must
name one day. Default:
.BR "%B %\-d, %Y (%A)" .
.TP
.I tag
strftime format of the dated tag;
.B \(dq\(dq
for none. Default:
.BR log/%Y/%m/%d .
.TP
.I template
The daily template: a name inside the templates directory, or an absolute or
.B ~/
path. Default:
.BR daily ,
and the built\-in layout when that file does not exist.
.TP
.I capture_section
A heading such as
.B \(dq## Inbox\(dq
that captures go under, matched ignoring case and made at the end of the note
when missing. Default: empty, the end of the note.
.TP
.I capture_format
The line a capture adds;
.BR {{time}} ,
.BR {{text}} ,
.B {{date}}
and
.B {{workspace}}
are filled in. Default:
.BR "* {{time}} {{text}}" .
.SS [templates]
.TP
.I dir
The templates directory
.B N
reads. Default:
.IR ~/.config/bjorn/templates .
.SS [[actions]]
An array of tables; see
.B ACTIONS
above for
.IR name ,
.IR command ,
.IR format ,
.IR confirm ,
.IR prompt ,
.IR interactive ,
.IR timeout ,
.IR output ,
.I section
and
.IR default .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B $VISUAL
The editor to run for
.BR e ,
used when
.I editor
is not set in the config file.
.TP
.B $EDITOR
The fallback when
.I editor
and
.B $VISUAL
are both unset. With none of the three,
.B vim
is used.
.TP
.B $BJORN_BEARCLI
The
.B bearcli
to run. It overrides the
.I bearcli
config key, which is otherwise the first thing tried; then
.BR $PATH ,
then inside Bear.app.
.TP
.B $BJORN_REMCTL
The
.B remctl
to run, overriding
.IR "[reminders] remctl" .
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME
The base for the config directory; without it,
.IR ~/.config .
.TP
.B $XDG_CACHE_HOME
The base for the cache directory; without it,
.IR ~/.cache .
.TP
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME
Used by
.I install.sh
for the binaries and the man page; without it,
.IR ~/.local/share .
.TP
.B $HOME
Resolves
.B ~
in the config file and in every default path.
.TP
.B $PATH
Searched for
.BR bearcli ,
.B remctl
and an editor command that is not an absolute path. It is also inherited by
every action.
.TP
.B $TMPDIR
Where the rendered note an action gets, an unwritten action output and an
editor's rescued text are kept.
.TP
.B $TERM_PROGRAM
Read once at start\-up to decide whether
.B "icon_style = \(dqauto\(dq"
draws Nerd Font glyphs.
.TP
.B $BJORN_FAKE_BEAR_STATE
.TQ
.B $BJORN_FAKE_REMCTL_STATE
Read by the bundled fakes that
.B \-\-demo
and the test suite run. They have no effect on a real run.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:\-~/.config}/bjorn/config.toml
The config file. Edited as text, so comments survive.
.TP
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:\-~/.config}/bjorn/themes/
Your own
.I .theme
files (see
.BR THEMES ).
.TP
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:\-~/.config}/bjorn/templates/
Note templates for
.BR N ,
and
.I daily.md
for
.BR D ,
unless
.I [templates] dir
names another directory.
.TP
.I ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:\-~/.cache}/bjorn/previews.json
The notes list's body previews, keyed by note id and modification stamp.
Derived state only: deleting it costs one slow start.
.TP
.I ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:\-~/.cache}/bjorn/previews\-demo.json
The same, for
.B \-\-demo
runs, so the sample library never stands in for the real one.
.TP
.I ${XDG_DATA_HOME:\-~/.local/share}/bjorn/bin/
Where
.I install.sh
puts the built binaries;
.I ~/bin/bjorn
is a symlink to the one here.
.TP
.I ${XDG_DATA_HOME:\-~/.local/share}/man/man1/bjorn.1
Where
.I install.sh
puts this page.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start scoped to a tag, so the tag tree, the counts, the searches and new notes
all stay inside it:
.PP
.EX
bjorn \-\-tag work
.EE
.PP
Try it without Bear, against the built\-in sample library:
.PP
.EX
bjorn \-\-demo
.EE
.PP
Send the note through an LLM CLI and append the answer under its own
heading. Give the command whatever option your CLI has for running with no
tools, since the note's text is the prompt:
.PP
.EX
[[actions]]
name = "Summarize"
command = 'llm "Summarize this note in five bullet points."'
output = "append"
section = "## Summary"
.EE
.PP
Make a note out of a GitHub issue; the prompt asks which one, and the
command's first line becomes the note's title:
.PP
.EX
[[actions]]
name = "Issue to note"
command = '''gh issue view "$BJORN_ACTION_INPUT" \-\-json title,body \e
  \-\-jq '"# \e(.title)\en\en\e(.body)"' '''
prompt = "Issue number"
output = "new\-note"
.EE
.PP
Publish a note to a Hugo site. Bjorn has no Hugo code of its own;
.I contrib/hugo\-publish
in the source tree is a script run as an action, which writes the note into the
site on your disk as a post and stops. A draft unless the entry says
.BR \-\-live ;
see
.I docs/actions.md
for its flags and what it refuses:
.PP
.EX
[[actions]]
name = "Hugo: save draft"
command = 'hugo\-publish \-\-site ~/sites/blog \-\-tag\-prefix blog/ \-\-bundle'
format = "textbundle"

[[actions]]
name = "Hugo: publish live"
command = 'hugo\-publish \-\-site ~/sites/blog \-\-tag\-prefix blog/ \-\-bundle \-\-live'
format = "textbundle"
confirm = true
.EE
.PP
Turn on daily notes and capture from the shell:
.PP
.EX
# ~/.config/bjorn/config.toml
[daily]
capture_section = "## Inbox"
.EE
.PP
.EX
bjorn capture "call Ana about the budget"
pbpaste | bjorn capture
bjorn capture \-\- "$text" </dev/null   # in a git hook
bjorn today
.EE
.PP
Choose a palette for one run, or for good:
.PP
.EX
bjorn \-\-list\-themes
bjorn \-\-theme nord
.EE
.PP
.EX
# ~/.config/bjorn/config.toml
theme = "tokyo\-night"
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. The interactive app exits 0 when you quit it, and
.B \-\-list\-themes
when it has printed the names.
.TP
.B 1
An error, with the reason on standard error; for example
.B \-\-theme
named a theme that does not exist, the config file does not parse, or the
terminal could not be put into raw mode.
For
.B capture
and
.BR today ,
also: daily notes are off, the text is blank or over 1 MiB, or
.B bearcli
failed.
.TP
.B 2
The command line itself was wrong \(em an unknown flag or a missing argument.
.PP
An action's own exit status is reported in a toast and never becomes Bjorn's.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bearcli (1),
.BR remctl (1),
.BR textutil (1),
.BR weasyprint (1)
.PP
Bjorn: https://github.com/7robots/bjorn
.br
Bear: https://bear.app
.br
remctl: https://github.com/7robots/remctl
.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
.B macOS only, and Bear must be installed.
Everything goes through
.BR bearcli ,
which ships inside Bear.app, and the editor, the pty, Apple Reminders and the
export writers are all built against macOS.
.IP \(bu 2
.B The binary makes no network calls.
There is no HTTP client in it. Anything that has to reach the network \(em a
model, an upload, a deploy, a commit \(em is an action you configure, running
with your credentials and your environment. Bjorn does not sandbox an action
and does not parse it.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Backlinks are capped.
.B L
reads at most 200 candidate notes per search and lists the first 1,000 links in
the note;
when the cap is hit the list says it may be incomplete.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Change detection is a poll.
There is no push from Bear, so a change made in Bear appears within
.I poll_seconds
rather than at once.
.B r
forces it.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Some things bearcli has no command for.
Permanent delete from the trash, renaming or deleting a tag, archiving from the
TUI, and saving attachments are all missing for that reason.
